1 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of draft PR 2815 on branch feat/ac-w1d-authserver, the assistant-channel authorization server (Wave 1 of issue 2799, contract in docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md on branch docs/prd-assistant-channel-w0 via PR 2802). No commits, no push, no review, no labels, no edits; tmp writes only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTIONS THAT MATTER: one, does every token this server mints carry an audience bound to the assistant channel and the assistant scope only, so it can never be replayed against the web session paths or the concierge paths; two, is PKCE enforced with S256 only, with the verifier checked against the stored challenge and single-use codes; three, does the code exchange refuse a redirect URI that was not registered byte for byte; four, does the refresh path rotate and revoke, so a stolen refresh token is detected on reuse; five, are the existing scope checks in apps/api/internal/middleware/auth.go untouched for every existing scope, in particular that an empty-scope token is still never gated; six, is there any path by which the new assistant scope reaches result-session GET, list or PATCH, or the pay-link creation, which the contract forbids. Write failing tests for anything that fails, run go test ./... in apps/api with the race flag, and paste real exit codes captured directly. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC W1D AUTHSERVER, with SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX and file and line citations for every finding. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/ac-w1d-authserver

2 | security-reviewer | SECURITY REVIEW of draft PR 2815 on branch feat/ac-w1d-authserver, the assistant-channel OAuth 2.1 authorization server. Read-only: no commits, no push, no review, no labels; tmp writes only. Record the PR head SHA at start and at report. Threat model: a hostile third-party assistant host, a stolen assistant access token, a stolen refresh token, an attacker who controls a registered client redirect URI, and an authorization code intercepted in transit. For each, state whether the implementation defeats it and cite the file and line that does so, or write the exploit steps if it does not. Also check: token and code storage never keeps plaintext secrets in Postgres, comparison of secrets is constant time, all new endpoints sit behind the existing rate limiter, the new endpoints appear in api.yaml or in the runtime allowlist of scripts/check-openapi-routes.py with a tracking issue, no new env var is undocumented in docs/operations/env-vars-api.md, and no logging call writes a token, code or verifier. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads SECURITY W1D AUTHSERVER, with a severity per finding and a final SAFE or BLOCK line. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/ac-w1d-authserver
